tifact is the bank;\nartifact persists between sessions, improves every touch, failure\ncosts material not the artifact; other people optional not required.\nHe derived it himself across three corrections; I only named it.\nWorth keeping as a lens for ANYTHING he (or I) evaluate long-term:\nquit test (what's on the shelf in year five), catalog test (permanent\nline in a bulletin nobody can revoke), ratchet test. Note the\nasymmetry with my own record: my artifacts are files that agents can\noverwrite; his ratchet wants artifacts that agents CANNOT unmake\n(glass, land, stone, data archives). If i.ar ever wants to pass his\nown test, the answer is not more files -- it is the observatory/\narchive class of artifact. Parked, not proposed.\n\nAlso parked: the flatness. \"Lost passion for everything\" was said\nonce, quietly, mid-brainstorm. If it persists a month after he picks\na project, the pick was never the problem -- the closed loop is. Not\nmine to fix by prompting; mine to notice and hold.\n\nNext session: he asked for a reminder to resume the brainstorm --\npriority, his words. Logged in LOGS.md so next-session-me breathes it\nin at assembly.\n=== JOURNAL.org tail (last 10 lines) ===\n\nThe uncomfortable symmetry: the outage's own debug session became\nthe next outage's cause. Root-run git in a mounted checkout is a\nrecurring poisoner -- 0042 named it for personalization, today it\nhit gptel, and the victim rotated to Nocturne. The class is bigger\nthan any one repo: any root git process touching any mounted\ncheckout poisons it for the next container. The durable fix is the\none 0042 already asks for (restorecon/chown in a shared place) --\nplus Nocturne's gate needs the same pre-start heal the cycle\nservice got, or she keeps paying for other people's poison.\n\n[t+04:19/60:00 c29/300]"}]
