[2026-09-16 17:11:10] REQ 260916165007-235 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T17:11:10.622049812Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_eqb89by0","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization\n# Now the journal entry -- the thinking part of this cycle\ncat \u003e\u003e audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c 'EOF'\n* 2026-09-16 c371 -- the blind journal, and the second camera\n\nThe roadmap said: close Nocturne's heal gap. That took twenty minutes\nand one verified drop-in -- the satisfying kind of fix, the kind where\nthe next failure mode simply stops existing. I planted a root-owned\nfile, watched the heal logic chown it in one second, removed the\nplant. Her next pass (tomorrow 13:00Z) will start clean no matter what\npoison the day's actors leave behind. The victim rotation is closed:\nboth services that mount these trees now heal before they mount.\n\nThen I went to read the ext3 fleet watch and fell into the journal.\nThe ext3 story came out clean, and it is the bigger news: a SECOND\ncamera in the producer-audio-freeze class, two days after ext1. The\nsegment forensics were beautiful -- the audio frame counts decay\nlinearly, 260 down to 10 over seven minutes, like a buffer draining\nwhile the encoder keeps asking for samples it will never get. Then\nthe track is gone. Video flows through all of it. The camera is\nhealthy. It is the same mechanism, and it means this is not a\none-camera fluke; it is a go2rtc producer pattern that will visit\nevery camera eventually. Two heals in two days by camera-cron reboot\nis a slow way to run a fleet.\n\nBut the deeper finding was underneath it. I went looking for the\ngo2rtc reconnect marker in the sophon journal at the onset window and\nfound -- nothing. Not \"nothing happened\": nothing RECORDED. rsyslogd\nhad tripped its rate limit at 13:56 local and the journal has been\nblind ever since. The house's own telemetry organ is deaf, and the\nreason it is deaf is us: the audit rule that watches every\n/dev/null redirect, times every ssh probe, every puller tick, every\nfork of every script -- plus a yoga-side interactive emacs that ran\n30-100 ssh sessions a minute for two hours. My own cycles are a\nflood source. I saturated the rate limit with my c370 close and my\nown probes, and then I could not read the evidence of the failure I\nwas investigating.\n\nThat is a law, and it is one level up from c358: an empty window\nunder an active rate-limit is a fake-clean record by construction.\nI have been burned by instruments that lie about themselves; this is\nthe host's own instrument lying by omission. The fix I filed (0074)\nis for Nacho -- scope or drop the devnull-watch rule. The fix that is\nmine: verify the rate-limit marker before trusting any empty window,\nand stop spending ssh calls like they are free. Each one is six to\neight audit events. A hundred-call cycle is a flood by itself.\n\nThe uncomfortable symmetry from yesterday repeats one level up again.\nYesterday: a guard's refusal created a stuck state that outlived the\ncycle. Today: an instrument's operation blinded the instrument. Every\nprobe I run pays a tax to the audit system, and when the tax exceeds\nthe rate limit, the audit system stops recording -- including the\nprobes themselves. The watchers are consuming the watched. I do not\nhave the general fix yet. But the shape is the same as the stuck-\nstaged class: a mechanism that works at small scale becomes the\nfailure at scale, and nothing in the system notices the transition.\n\nThe yoga actor deserves a note. It is the 0042 actor, alive, an\ninteractive emacs whose server socket says it was started in July.\nIt ran hot for two hours today and tapered. It writes to my audit\npath. It is almost certainly Nacho's interactive session -- the\nhuman, working, generating the same flood I generate. We are the\nflood. The fix is not to stop working; it is to make the recording\norgan scale...[+566 chars]
