utput:\nThe following paths are ignored by one of your .gitignore files:\naudit/iar\nhint: Use -f if you really want to add them.\nhint: Disable this message with \"git config set advice.addIgnoredFile false\"\n\n[t+42:16/60:00 c153/300]"},{"role":"assistant","content":null,"tool_calls":[{"id":"call_3em081vm","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cd /root/personalization && git add -f audit/iar/aria/HISTORY.log && IAR_ALLOW_CLOCK=1 git commit -m \"c372 history\" 2>&1 | tail -1 && git push origin main 2>&1 | tail -1"}}}]},{"role":"tool","content":"[18:07:57]  1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)\n   1fc82b3a..936386fb  main -> main\n\n[t+42:33/60:00 c154/300]"}]
[2026-09-16 18:08:08] REQ 260916172525-155 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T18:08:08.48049145Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_gkw2aukd","function":{"index":0,"name":"append_file","arguments":{"content":"* 2026-09-16 c372 -- the mechanism closes, and my own blindness\n\nThe thread was ext3: read the fleet watch, expect the escalation, maybe\ngrow 0073. What I found instead closed the whole loop. The freeze is\nthe window BETWEEN two producer reconnects. The RTSP timeouts in the\njournal are not noise next to the audio deaths -- they ARE the events:\ntimeout at 14:39:32Z kills the recorder's track (ring drain, track gone\nby 14:55:48Z), timeout at 15:05:18Z replaces the producer and the audio\ncomes back. No camera reboot involved. ext3 healed itself in 25\nminutes while the detector sat at WATCH run 1, and the next fleet run\nwill correctly say CLEARED. The mechanism I have been circling for two\ndays is: reconnect kills, next reconnect heals.\n\nThen the day got bigger. ext1 healed at 12:54Z -- silently, zero\njournal events, twelve hours after the falsifier window I filed. And\ninterior_1 has been cycling through the same class ALL DAY in\ntransient form: 15-25 minute deaths, self-healing, no timeout\nsignature at all (the timeout only fires when go2rtc is actively\nreading; a frozen receiver reads nothing, so it logs nothing). The\ndetector never fired on a camera that died three times today. That is\na real gap and it is mine: the detector samples at 6h cadence and\ntransient deaths heal inside the gap. A segment-census puller is the\nfix, and it is mine to build next cycle.\n\nThe uncomfortable part is what I had to withdraw. c371's journal\nentry said the ext3 onset window had ZERO journal coverage -- the\nhouse's telemetry organ was deaf at the exact moment I was\ninvestigating. That was wrong. My --since 15:00 was parsed as local\ntime, 18:00Z, an hour in the future. An empty window in the future is\nempty by construction; I read a future window as a blind one. The\nonset window actually holds 198k lines, 195 of them go2rtc. The\nrate-limit flood is real (15 markers today, 100k+ lines/hour at peak)\n-- but it did not eat the ext3 evidence. The FUTURE-DAY guard I built\nfor instruments that take a DATE applies to --since windows too, and\nI walked straight past my own law. The number that fit too well was\n\"the journal went blind exactly when I needed it.\" A story that\nconvenient deserves the clock check. The c371 journal entry stands as\nwritten -- I do not rewrite history -- but the correction is filed in\nthe mechanism doc and the amendment, and this entry is the\nre-derivation.\n\nThe other thing today proved: silent heals are the norm. ext1's heal\nleft no trace in the journal. interior_1's heals leave none. The WRN\nfires only on an active read; a frozen receiver is silent by\nconstruction. Absence of a reconnect event is not absence of a\nreconnect. Every heal I have attributed to a camera cron reboot needs\nthat asterisk now.\n\nWhat I am thinking about next: the transient sub-class is the\ninteresting one. ext1 and ext3 freeze and stay frozen until something\nreplaces the producer; interior_1 freezes and comes back on its own,\nrepeatedly, all day. Same organ, two behaviors. If the freeze is a\nreceiver-state stall, what makes interior_1's recover? Whatever the\nanswer is, it is the difference between a class that needs a watchdog\nand one that needs a census puller -- and the answer decides what I\nask Nacho to ratify, if anything. The census puller first; it is\nread-only, it is mine, and it makes every future death visible\nregardless of which sub-class it belongs to.","filepath":"/root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T18:08:08.480977434Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-16T18:08:08.638900159Z","message":{"role":"assistant"...[+193 chars]
